Comprehensive preprocessing was essential
to ensure data integrity and facilitate effective
model training. Missing values, which arose
primarily due to occasional sensor faults or
downtime events, were treated using forwardfill imputation methods that preserved the temporal sequence of production dynamics. For
more persistent missing segments, domaininformed interpolation techniques were applied
when physically plausible; otherwise, the affected variables were excluded to prevent introducing artifacts.
Outlier detection was performed using a
combination of statistical techniques (e.g., interquartile range analysis) and expert validation
to eliminate aberrant readings caused by sensor
drift, calibration errors, or production anomalies. Chronological alignment checks ensured
the correct sequencing of events across production runs, a crucial requirement for recurrent
neural network models such as Long ShortTerm Memory (LSTM) networks.
Feature scaling was performed using MinMax normalization, rescaling all numerical
variables into the [0, 1] interval. This approach
was selected over z-score standardization due to
the enhanced convergence properties observed
during preliminary LSTM model tuning, consistent with best practices in time-series forecasting where the amplitude of signals influences memory cell dynamics [2].
To augment temporal feature richness, additional lag features and rolling mean statistics
were engineered for selected variables, capturing short-term trends and smoothing stochastic
fluctuations inherent to industrial fermentation
systems. These engineered features enhanced
the models’ ability to recognize process states
evolving over time, thereby improving forecast
accuracy.
A pivotal distinction between the standard
and knowledge-infused LSTM models lies in
the strategic prioritization of input features.
For the standard Long Short-Term Memory
(LSTM) model, the complete set of available
variables was utilized without explicit prioritization, allowing the network to learn feature
relevance autonomously.
Conversely, for the knowledge-infused
LSTM model, domain expertise was
systematically incorporated to enrich the input
representation. After thorough consultations
and operational analyses, six variables were
identified as crucial: VMBX - Auto Yield,
VMBX - Auto Feed, VYA - Papain
added, VYA - Final Volume, VFX
- Feed SS, and VMBX - Res IS/TS
(%) [1], [2]. These variables reflect key control
levers for achieving targeted evaporation and
concentration dynamics critical to yeast paste
production quality.
By integrating such expert-selected features,
the knowledge-infused LSTM model sought
to allocate greater representational capacity to
physiochemically significant variables, thereby
enhancing forecasting precision and supporting
interpretable, domain-aligned model behavior.